                <pb facs="0550" n="522" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v21/pg522"/>

                <head>333. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in the United Kingdom<anchor n="0"
                        xml:id="d333-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1962-11-29T18:11:00-05:00">November 29, 1962,
                            6:11 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>2830. Inform appropriate UK officials that US
                    representatives will wish discuss during December 4-5 African talks possibility
                    of more effective UK and US roles re Southern
                    Rhodesia if Whitehead wins substantial
                    victory in December 14 elections. US believes that in new situation following
                    coming into force of new constitution and elections, UK and US may be able help bridge gap between Whitehead’s government and African nationalists.
                    Important element of such effort would be provision increased training
                    opportunities to prepare more Africans for participation in government
                    activities. This should ease way to more expeditious expansion of African
                    participation in government which US considers essential to orderly political
                    progress in Southern Rhodesia and to continuation of at least economic ties
                    between three components of Federation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Rusk</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d333-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d333-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 745C.00/11-2962. Confidential; Limit
                    Distribution. Drafted by Mathews on November 23; cleared by Burdett, Sisco, Furnas, and McGhee (in substance); and approved by Tasca. Repeated to Salisbury.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
